;; HOW OFTEN A RESIDENT BOT ASKS AGAIN — decided from what its last run found.
;;
;; Sibling of `routine_core.kotoba` (when a recorded workflow may run again),
;; not a second scheduler. The clock, the queue and the writing down stay in
;; `bots.clj`; the only judgement here is the length of the next gap.
;;
;; ## Why a fixed cadence was wrong in both directions
;;
;; Until 2026-08-29 `bots.clj` computed `next-run-at` as `now + cadence`, with
;; `cadence` a constant per role in loop-yakuwari's `workforce.edn`. Measured
;; that day: resident runs took p50 174s against `max-active 2`, so the fleet
;; can serve roughly 993 runs a day, while 126 Bots on a 15-minute cadence ask
;; for 12,096. Twelve times over, the number in the table stops describing
;; anything -- the queue decides, and every Bot waits the same ~3 hours whether
;; it had work or not.
;;
;; The scarce thing is not the interval. It is the two slots. A Bot that found
;; nothing and a Bot that changed something are asking for the same slot at the
;; same rate, so the one with work waits behind the one without.
;;
;; ## The three answers, and why the third is not the second
;;
;;   produced-change  the run changed something -> ask again at the floor.
;;                    There is work here; stay as fast as the operator allows.
;;
;;   no-op            the run completed and found nothing to do -> double,
;;                    up to `ceiling`. This is EVIDENCE about the work, and
;;                    backing off hands the slot to a Bot that has some.
;;
;;   anything else    the run did not finish -- provider 503, timeout, a code
;;                    this file does not know -> double, but only up to
;;                    `retry-ceiling`, which the host sets far below `ceiling`.
;;
;; The third case exists because a run that could not execute MEASURED NOTHING
;; about whether there was work. Folding it into `no-op` would let one hour of
;; provider outage push every Bot to a daily interval, and they would still be
;; slow a day after the provider came back. That is the same failure this
;; workspace keeps finding elsewhere: a check that could not run returning the
;; value of a check that ran and found nothing.
;;
;; An unrecognised code takes the third branch on purpose. `no-op` is the only
;; input that earns the long back-off, and it has to be said explicitly.
;;
;; ## Shape
;;
;; Five `:i64` in, one `:i64` out: native-crossable, no records, no strings.
;; Clamping rather than refusing, because there is no sane way to fail a
;; schedule -- a Bot with an unusable interval must still get a next time, and
;; the clamp is visible in the returned number.

(ns cloud.itonami.app.workforce-cadence
  (:export [outcome-produced-change outcome-no-op outcome-unavailable
            next-interval-minutes]))

(defn outcome-produced-change [] :i64 0)
(defn outcome-no-op [] :i64 1)
(defn outcome-unavailable [] :i64 2)

(defn at-least [a :i64 b :i64] :i64
  (if (< a b) b a))

(defn at-most [a :i64 b :i64] :i64
  (if (> a b) b a))

(defn next-interval-minutes
  [floor :i64 ceiling :i64 retry-ceiling :i64 current :i64 outcome :i64] :i64
  (let [f (at-least floor 1)
        c (at-least ceiling f)
        rc (at-least (at-most retry-ceiling c) f)
        cur (at-least (at-most current c) f)
        doubled (* cur 2)]
    (if (= outcome 0)
      f
      (if (= outcome 1)
        (at-most doubled c)
        (at-most doubled rc)))))
